1
00:00:00,480 --> 00:00:01,833
To help us walk through

2
00:00:02,780 --> 00:00:06,160
what they're doing now, what the crew
is up to, we're joined right now by

3
00:00:06,560 --> 00:00:08,480
Colonel Jim Dutton,
who's one of our astronauts,

4
00:00:08,480 --> 00:00:11,657
part of the 2004 class.
Jim, thank you for joining us.

5
00:00:11,920 --> 00:00:13,640
It's great to be here, Allard. Thanks.

6
00:00:13,640 --> 00:00:14,360
Well, if you wouldn't mind,

7
00:00:14,360 --> 00:00:16,520
we've got a couple minutes
before we see the crew,

8
00:00:16,520 --> 00:00:18,680
why don't you walk us through
you?

9
00:00:20,380 --> 00:00:23,360
Okay. A little about me:
I grew up in Eugene, Oregon.

10
00:00:23,360 --> 00:00:27,831
I joined the Astronaut Corps about four
years ago, as you said, May of 2004,

11
00:00:28,040 --> 00:00:31,040
with the
class we affectionately call the Peacocks.

12
00:00:32,520 --> 00:00:34,780
And my background is with the Air Force.

13
00:00:34,780 --> 00:00:39,780
I was a test pilot, and before that I flew
as an operational pilot in the F-15 Eagle.

14
00:00:40,620 --> 00:00:43,120
I have to—you opened it up, I have to ask.

15
00:00:43,120 --> 00:00:46,880
This is what happened: the classes give
themselves different names, and Peacock.

16
00:00:46,880 --> 00:00:47,880
Why a Peacock?

17
00:00:47,940 --> 00:00:50,060
Well, we didn't give ourselves that name.

18
00:00:50,060 --> 00:00:52,840
Although I'll say
we took it over an alternative

19
00:00:52,840 --> 00:00:55,370
that we preferred
Peacock to the other one.

20
00:00:56,320 --> 00:00:57,980
But
when we first came in,

21
00:00:57,980 --> 00:00:59,040
we were told as a class

22
00:00:59,040 --> 00:01:02,800
that we would not fly on the shuttle,
based on the way things were going

23
00:01:02,800 --> 00:01:05,519
and how it looked
with the number of crew members

24
00:01:05,519 --> 00:01:07,640
or astronauts who had not flown yet.

25
00:01:07,640 --> 00:01:12,000
And so there was also a lot of pomp
and circumstance about our arrival

26
00:01:12,000 --> 00:01:14,480
because of the new vision
for going back to the Moon.

27
00:01:14,480 --> 00:01:17,060
And
so we were labeled Peacocks

28
00:01:17,060 --> 00:01:21,740
because we're all show and we don't fly.
So I think we can, you know,

29
00:01:21,740 --> 00:01:24,880
take pride in that now that we've got
our first classmate on this flight,

30
00:01:24,880 --> 00:01:27,700
Aki Hoshide,
a Japanese astronaut,

31
00:01:28,320 --> 00:01:32,480
and we're going to start seeing a lot
more of our classmates on shuttle flights.

32
00:01:32,480 --> 00:01:35,140
And they're trying to get actually
all the rookies in the office,

33
00:01:35,140 --> 00:01:36,860
the unflown astronauts,
up in

34
00:01:38,100 --> 00:01:40,277
one mode or another, most on shuttle,

35
00:01:41,360 --> 00:01:42,500
before we retire the shuttle.

36
00:01:42,500 --> 00:01:45,760
As a way to get experience to be ready
for, obviously, the Constellation program,

37
00:01:45,760 --> 00:01:47,300
the Orion capsule and spacecraft.

38
00:01:47,300 --> 00:01:48,960
Exactly. We're going to have a five-year,

39
00:01:48,960 --> 00:01:52,680
roughly, delay between shuttle
and bringing Orion online,

40
00:01:52,820 --> 00:01:57,020
so we want to carry as much experience
as we can over into Constellation.

41
00:01:57,420 --> 00:01:59,320
As a matter of fact, NASA is also

42
00:01:59,720 --> 00:02:00,520
right now actively looking,

43
00:02:00,520 --> 00:02:03,780
at least for another month or so,
for a 2009 class.

44
00:02:03,960 --> 00:02:04,640
That's correct.

45
00:02:04,640 --> 00:02:08,160
I think pretty probably safe to say
that that class will not fly on a shuttle.

46
00:02:08,160 --> 00:02:11,920
That's true. They are being brought
in to actually fly on station

47
00:02:11,920 --> 00:02:13,920
during the interim before Constellation

48
00:02:13,920 --> 00:02:16,100
and then obviously carry
on into Constellation.

49
00:02:16,100 --> 00:02:17,520
So,
yeah, it's very exciting.

50
00:02:17,520 --> 00:02:19,980
It'll be a five-year gap,
which is a very long gap,

51
00:02:19,980 --> 00:02:23,745
and it's going to be great to have
some new folks in the office.
